{
  "id": "V029",
  "description": "Rung 2 compares the approval FRACTION (yes among attendees / attendee_votes), not the raw yes-vote count: the movie with fewer yes-votes but higher approval wins the cycle",
  "spec_clause": "Phase 2 / Tally: 'Cycles are possible (A beats B beats C beats A) and must be handled rather than left to crash. Resolve in order: 1 Copeland score, 2 Approval count, 3 Rotation fairness, 4 Shortest runtime, 5 Seeded random.' + Phase 1 / Eligibility: 'approval(movie) = yes_votes among attendees / attendee_votes(movie)'",
  "rationale": "5 attendees. m1: 5 rows, 4 yes -> coverage 1.00, yes 4, approval 4/5 = 0.80. m2: 3 rows, 3 yes -> coverage 3/5 = 0.60 (at floor), yes 3, approval 3/3 = 1.00. m3: 5 rows, 3 yes -> yes 3, approval 3/5 = 0.60. All eligible and clearing; ranked m1 (4 yes), then m2 vs m3 tie on 3 yes broken by approval (1.00 > 0.60) -> m1, m2, m3. Round robin: m1 vs m2 = 3 (u1,u2,u3) to 2 (u4,u5) -> m1; m2 vs m3 = 3 to 2 -> m2; m1 vs m3 = 2 (u1,u2) to 3 (u3,u4,u5) -> m3. Cycle m1 > m2 > m3 > m1, no Condorcet winner. Copeland m1 = m2 = m3 = 1 -> tie. Rung 2 approval fraction: m2 1.00 > m1 0.80 > m3 0.60 -> winner m2. An implementation that read 'Approval count' as the raw yes-vote count would pick m1 (4 yes vs m2's 3) - this vector distinguishes the two readings.",
